    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Does an Electric Car Actually Save You Money?\"\nsubtitle: \"EVs are much cheaper to run. They're not cheaper to own, at least not new. Here's the math, per year.\"\nauthor: \"SixtyEight95\"\ndate: \"2026-11-16\"\ncategories: [cars, cost of living, energy]\nimage: infographic.png\n---\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n:::\n\n\nElectric car fans say you'll save a fortune on gas. Skeptics say EVs cost more and lose value fast. They're\nboth partly right. To settle it, we compared the **full cost of owning** an EV, a gas car, and a hybrid of the\n**same size and class**, **per year**.\n\n## The short answer\n\n::: {.callout-note appearance=\"simple\"}\nAn EV saves you about **$1,728 a year** on fuel and maintenance (for a medium sedan).\nBut it loses value so much faster that a new EV costs **$3,080 more per year**\nto own than a comparable gas sedan. In SUVs, it's roughly a tie. And in every class, a **hybrid** was cheapest.\n:::\n\n## How we compared them\n\nWe used AAA's 2026 *Your Driving Costs* study, which averages the top-selling 2026 models in each class and\nadds up everything it costs to own one for five years at 15,000 miles a year:\n\n- **Fuel or charging**\n- **Maintenance and repairs**, including tires\n- **Depreciation**: how much value the car loses\n- **Insurance** (full coverage)\n- **License, registration, and taxes**, including the extra EV fees most states now charge\n- **Finance charges**: interest on the car loan\n\nThat puts every vehicle on the same footing: **dollars per year.**\n\n## The results\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"plotly html-widget html-fill-item\" id=\"htmlwidget-08b3cb89a7fb1c544b4f\" style=\"width:100%;height:417px;\"></div>\n<script type=\"application/json\" data-for=\"htmlwidget-08b3cb89a7fb1c544b4f\">{\"x\":{\"data\":[{\"orientation\":\"v\",\"width\":[13662,11888,14332,17414],\"base\":[4.1416666666666666,3.1416666666666666,2.1416666666666666,1.1416666666666666],\"x\