emode\":\"pixel\"},{\"type\":\"rect\",\"fillcolor\":null,\"line\":{\"color\":null,\"width\":0,\"linetype\":[]},\"yref\":\"paper\",\"xref\":\"paper\",\"x0\":0,\"x1\":0.49592302674494454,\"y0\":0,\"y1\":0.48351090816844239},{\"type\":\"rect\",\"fillcolor\":null,\"line\":{\"color\":null,\"width\":0,\"linetype\":[]},\"yref\":\"paper\",\"xref\":\"paper\",\"x0\":0,\"x1\":0.49592302674494454,\"y0\":0,\"y1\":23.37899543378996,\"yanchor\":0.48351090816844239,\"ysizemode\":\"pixel\"},{\"type\":\"rect\",\"fillcolor\":null,\"line\":{\"color\":null,\"width\":0,\"linetype\":[]},\"yref\":\"paper\",\"xref\":\"paper\",\"x0\":0.5040769732550554,\"x1\":1,\"y0\":0,\"y1\":0.48351090816844239},{\"type\":\"rect\",\"fillcolor\":null,\"line\":{\"color\":null,\"width\":0,\"linetype\":[]},\"yref\":\"paper\",\"xref\":\"paper\",\"x0\":0.5040769732550554,\"x1\":1,\"y0\":0,\"y1\":23.37899543378996,\"yanchor\":0.48351090816844239,\"ysizemode\":\"pixel\"}],\"xaxis2\":{\"type\":\"linear\",\"autorange\":false,\"range\":[-728.70000000000005,15302.700000000001],\"tickmode\":\"array\",\"ticktext\":[\"$0\",\"$5,000\",\"$10,000\",\"$15,000\"],\"tickvals\":[0,5000,10000,15000],\"categoryorder\":\"array\",\"categoryarray\":[\"$0\",\"$5,000\",\"$10,000\",\"$15,000\"],\"nticks\":null,\"ticks\":\"\",\"tickcolor\":null,\"ticklen\":3.6529680365296811,\"tickwidth\":0,\"showticklabels\":true,\"tickfont\":{\"color\":\"rgba(77,77,77,1)\",\"family\":\"\",\"size\":11.68949771689498},\"tickangle\":-0,\"showline\":false,\"linecolor\":null,\"linewidth\":0,\"showgrid\":true,\"domain\":[0.5040769732550554,1],\"gridcolor\":\"rgba(235,235,235,1)\",\"gridwidth\":0.66417600664176002,\"zeroline\":false,\"anchor\":\"y2\",\"title\":\"\",\"hoverformat\":\".2f\"},\"yaxis2\":{\"type\":\"linear\",\"autorange\":false,\"range\":[0.40000000000000002,2.6000000000000001],\"tickmode\":\"array\",\"ticktext\":[\"Electric\",\"Gas\"],\"tickvals\":[1,2],\"categoryorder\":\"array\",\"categoryarray\":[\"Electric\",\"Gas\"],\"nticks\":null,\"ticks\":\"\",\"tickcolor\":null,\"ticklen\":3.6529680365296811,\"tickwidth\":0,\"showticklabels\":true,\"tickfont\":{\"color\":\"rgba(77,77,77,1)\",\"family\":\"\",\"size\":11.68949771689498},\"tickangle\":-0,\"showline\":false,\"linecolor\":null,\"linewidth\":0,\"showgrid\":true,\"domain\":[0,0.48351090816844239],\"gridcolor\":\"rgba(235,235,235,1)\",\"gridwidth\":0.66417600664176002,\"zeroline\":false,\"anchor\":\"x\",\"title\":\"\",\"hoverformat\":\".2f\"},\"showlegend\":true,\"legend\":{\"bgcolor\":null,\"bordercolor\":null,\"borderwidth\":0,\"font\":{\"color\":\"rgba(0,0,0,1)\",\"family\":\"\",\"size\":11.68949771689498},\"title\":{\"text\":\"\",\"font\":{\"color\":\"rgba(0,0,0,1)\",\"family\":\"\",\"size\":14.611872146118724}},\"orientation\":\"h\",\"y\":1.1200000000000001},\"hovermode\":\"closest\",\"barmode\":\"relative\"},\"config\":{\"doubleClick\":\"reset\",\"modeBarButtonsToAdd\":[\"hoverclosest\",\"hovercompare\"],\"showSendToCloud\":false,\"displayModeBar\":false},\"source\":\"A\",\"attrs\":{\"4fdc169d4e54\":{\"x\":{},\"y\":{},\"fill\":{},\"text\":{},\"type\":\"bar\"}},\"cur_data\":\"4fdc169d4e54\",\"visdat\":{\"4fdc169d4e54\":[\"function (y) \",\"x\"]},\"highlight\":{\"on\":\"plotly_click\",\"persistent\":false,\"dynamic\":false,\"selectize\":false,\"opacityDim\":0.20000000000000001,\"selected\":{\"opacity\":1},\"debounce\":0},\"shinyEvents\":[\"plotly_hover\",\"plotly_click\",\"plotly_selected\",\"plotly_relayout\",\"plotly_brushed\",\"plotly_brushing\",\"plotly_clickannotation\",\"plotly_doubleclick\",\"plotly_deselect\",\"plotly_afterplot\",\"plotly_sunburstclick\"],\"base_url\":\"https://plot.ly\"},\"evals\":[],\"jsHooks\":[]}</script>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe biggest single cost of owning *any* new car is **depreciation**, the value it loses while you own it. For\nEVs, it's brutal. AAA puts a new electric medium sedan's depreciation at **$6,786 a year**, versus\n**$3,330** for gas. Fast-improving battery technology and price cuts on new EVs make used\nones worth less.\n\nEVs also tend to cost more to **insure** (they're pricier to repair), most states now charge an extra\n**annual EV registration fee** (often $100 to $250) to make up for lost gas-tax revenue, and a pricier car means\nmore **loan interest**. And as of September 30, 2025, the **$7,500 federal EV tax credit is gone**.\n\n## So who should buy an EV?\n\nThe numbers point to a few clear cases where an EV makes financial sense:\n\n- **You can charge at home.** That's where the fuel savings come from.\n- **You drive a lot.** More miles means more fuel savings to offset the extra ownership costs.\n- **You keep cars a long time.** Depreciation is heaviest in the first few years. Keep the car 10 years\n  and the per-year cost of that early drop shrinks.\n- **You buy used.** The first owner already took the big depreciation hit. A used EV can capture the\n  running-cost savings without paying for the steepest part of the value drop.\n- **You're shopping SUVs, not sedans.** The gap is smallest there.\n\nIf saving money is the top priority and none of those fit, the data say: **buy a hybrid.** It beat both EVs\nand gas cars in every class AAA measured.\n\n## The fine print\n\n- **These are averages** across top-selling models. A specific EV with a big discount, or a gas guzzler, can\n  change the answer.\n- **Five-year ownership** is AAA's assumption. Longer ownership generally helps EVs.\n- **Electricity and gas prices vary a lot by state.** Cheap power and expensive gas favor EVs.\n- **Public charging prices** vary by network and membership; ours is a typical 2026 figure.\n- This post is about money. **Whether EVs are better for the environment** is a separate question, and our\n  follow-up post tackles it.\n\n## The bottom line\n\nEVs really are cheaper to run. But \"cheaper to run\" isn't \"cheaper to own.\" Buying new, without the old\nfederal tax credit, an EV costs about the same as a gas SUV and more than a gas sedan, because it loses value so\nfast. If you can charge at home, drive a lot, keep cars for years, or buy used, the math tilts toward electric.\nOtherwise, a hybrid is the money winner.\n\n---\n\n**How we did this.** Annual costs are from AAA's *Your Driving Costs* 2026 study (EV, gas, and hybrid\ncomparison for medium sedans, compact SUVs, medium SUVs, and pickups; 15,000 miles a year, five-year ownership,\nfull-coverage insurance). Home electricity is the BLS average U.S. price per kWh over the last 12 months, via\n[FRED](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/APU000072610). The public fast-charging price is a typical 2026 figure from\nindustry price trackers.\n[AAA Your Driving Costs 2026](https://newsroom.aaa.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/8-YDC-Brochure_2026-1.pdf)\n",
